{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>When there is to a man.</b> It is juxtaposed here to say that if he is wayward and rebellious, they shall not wait for him (to prove his case) but rather put him to death." "<b>A wayward and rebellious son.</b> We learned there: from when is a wayward and rebellious son liable? From when he eats a tarteimar of meat and drinks half a log of Italian wine. Rabbi Yosi says: a maneh of meat and a log of wine. Rabbi Zeira said: I do not know what a tarteimar is, but since Rabbi Yosi doubled the wine, he also doubled the meat — so a tarteimar is half a maneh. Here Rashi explained: on account of his end he is put to death, etc. — meaning, he will rob people and kill them even on the Sabbath, and incur stoning; analogous to one who pursues his fellow to kill him, where permission is given to every person to save the pursued one with the life of the pursuer, and it is a commandment to kill him on account of the future — that he intends to kill his fellow if they leave him alone, even though he has not yet done anything. And do not answer me from Yishmael, that he was not judged except according to his deeds at that time, for Yishmael was righteous at that time despite what he was destined to be wicked in his end, as explained in the portion of VaYera." "<b>Rebellious.</b> From the root of קנטר, strife, like מורת רוח."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The compiler cites Rashi by name here; rendered as written. The phrase 'on account of his end he is put to death' paraphrases Sanhedrin 72a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:46.806155833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}